      <h1 class="text-balance">Chair Headrest for Tall People: Leap V2, Gesture, and Aeron (2026)</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg md:text-xl text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto animate-slide-up">
        Why premium chairs stop at your shoulder blades — and which aftermarket headrests actually close the gap
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="container-article py-12 md:py-16">
    <Byline publishedDate="2026-08-04" updatedDate="2026-08-04" />

    <div class="bg-secondary/40 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-4 mb-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The Steelcase Leap V2, Steelcase Gesture, and Herman Miller Aeron all ship without a headrest, and their backrests top out at roughly 23"–25" above the seat pan. At 6'2"–6'6", the occiput sits about 33"–35" above the seat — an 8"–11" gap with no support. Only the Herman Miller Aeron has aftermarket headrests in stock (Engineered Now, Atlas; $140–$190). The Leap V2 unit is real but sold out at every verified seller as of August 2026, and it does not fit the Leap Plus. There is no aftermarket headrest for the Steelcase Gesture at all; Steelcase sells that headrest only bundled at time of chair purchase.</p>
    </div>

    <Disclosure />

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <section>
        <h2>Why Premium Task Chairs Ship Without Headrests</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>TCA's headrest analysis reports that the Steelcase Leap V2, Steelcase Gesture, and Herman Miller Aeron all ship without a headrest, with backrests topping out around 23 to 25 inches above the seat pan. At 6 feet 2 inches to 6 feet 6 inches the occiput sits roughly 33 to 35 inches above the seat, leaving an 8 to 11 inch gap with no support. Of the three, only the Aeron currently has a headrest available.</p>
        <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. The chair in my apartment is a Steelcase Gesture, and it has no headrest. Neither does a standard Leap V2. Neither does any Herman Miller Aeron, in any size, in any generation. That is not an oversight.</p>
        <p>Premium task chairs are engineered around an upright work posture. The design assumption is that the user sits between 90 and 110 degrees of trunk angle, with the head balanced over the shoulders and the cervical spine loaded almost entirely in compression. In that posture a headrest does nothing useful — the head is already in equilibrium, and a pad behind the skull would push the head forward rather than support it. Manufacturers leave it off to protect the posture they designed for.</p>
        <p>The problem is that the assumption breaks twice. It breaks when the user reclines past about 110 degrees, which most people do for reading, calls, and thinking. And it breaks structurally for tall users, because the backrest was scaled to a torso far shorter than a 6'2"-plus frame. For the tall user, the backrest stops well before the top of the thoracic spine, so even upright there is nothing behind the upper back to work against.</p>
        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm">
          <strong>Note on testing:</strong> I have personally used and tested only the Steelcase Gesture. I have not sat in a Leap V2 or an Aeron, and I have not installed any headrest in this guide. Every headrest assessment below is based on published manufacturer dimensions, seller compatibility documentation, aggregated reports from r/ergonomics and r/officechair, and engineering analysis of mounting geometry and load path.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Geometry: Where Your Neck Sits vs. Where the Backrest Stops</h2>
        <p>This is a subtraction problem, and it's worth doing before spending $190 on an accessory. Two numbers matter: how far above the seat pan the backrest reaches, and how far above the seat pan the back of your skull sits.</p>

        <h3>Seated landmark heights for tall users</h3>
        <p>Seated anthropometry scales roughly linearly with stature. Erect sitting height — seat pan to crown of the head — runs about 0.52 times standing height in adults with typical proportions. The occiput, the bony ridge at the base of the skull where a headrest should make contact, sits approximately 5"–6" below the crown. Acromion height, the top of the shoulder, runs about 24"–26" above the seat in this range.</p>

        <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border my-6">
