                <td class="p-3 text-center">~9"</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>All three land within about two inches of each other, and all three stop at or just below shoulder height for a 6'4" user. That is the whole story in one line: <strong>the backrest ends at the shoulder blades, and everything above the shoulder blades is cantilevered.</strong></p>

        <p>At 6'4" in my Gesture, this is exactly what I feel. The top edge of the backrest contacts me somewhere across the upper part of my shoulder blades. Everything above that — the last few inches of thoracic spine, the whole cervical spine, and about eleven pounds of head — has no structure behind it. Upright, that's manageable. Reclined, it is the reason I stop reclining.</p>

        <!-- Citation capsule -->
        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm italic">The Steelcase Leap V2, Steelcase Gesture, and Herman Miller Aeron all ship with backrests topping out roughly 23 to 25 inches above the seat pan, which lands at or just below shoulder height for users between 6'2" and 6'6". Seated occiput height at those statures falls between roughly 33 and 35 inches above the seat pan, leaving an 8 to 11 inch span of unsupported cervical spine. Aftermarket headrests from Engineered Now and Atlas close that span on the Herman Miller Aeron, while the Steelcase Leap V2 unit was sold out at every verified seller as of August 2026 and no aftermarket headrest exists for the Steelcase Gesture or the Steelcase Leap Plus.</p>

        <p>For the underlying dimensional math on seat height, seat depth, and backrest height by user stature, see the <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">correct chair dimensions guide by height →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Does a Headrest Actually Do Anything, or Is It Just a Recline Accessory?</h2>
        <p>Mostly it's a recline accessory — and that is not a criticism. The value shows up when the trunk angle opens past about 110 degrees, because that is when the head stops balancing over the shoulders and starts hanging off the front of the cervical spine.</p>
        <p>The load numbers are worth knowing. An adult head weighs roughly 10–12 lb in neutral position. A 2014 analysis in <em>Surgical Technology International</em> by Kenneth Hansraj modeled cervical spine loading as a function of head flexion and found effective load rising to approximately 27 lb at 15 degrees, 40 lb at 30 degrees, 49 lb at 45 degrees, and 60 lb at 60 degrees of forward tilt. The mechanism is a moment arm: as the skull's center of mass translates forward of the C7 vertebra, the posterior neck extensors must generate increasing torque to hold it.</p>
        <p>Reclining without head support produces a mirrored version of the same problem. If the trunk drops back to 120 degrees and the head stays level with the monitor, the neck is now in sustained flexion relative to the torso — and the cervical extensors hold that position isometrically for as long as you stay there. A headrest at the correct height removes that torque entirely by transferring head weight into the chair frame.</p>
        <p>Upright, at 90–100 degrees, a headrest does close to nothing. Buy one for the reclined half of your day, not the typing half.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Aftermarket Headrest Comparison</h2>
        <p>Three products with live Amazon listings cover the Aeron. The Leap V2, Leap Plus, and Gesture rows are included because the honest answer for those chairs is that nothing is currently buyable, and a guide that hides that is wasting your time. The fitment column matters because headrest compatibility is generation-specific and the single most common cause of returns in this category.</p>

