      <section>
        <h2>Herman Miller Aeron Headrests</h2>
        <p>Herman Miller has never manufactured an Aeron headrest, in any size or generation. That is a deliberate design position, and it means every Aeron headrest on the market is third-party. Two makers dominate: Engineered Now and Atlas.</p>
        <p>The critical fitment variable is generation. The Aeron Classic ran from 1994 to 2016; the Remastered Aeron launched in 2016 with a revised back frame, different posture-fit hardware, and altered top-rail geometry. Headrests are not cross-compatible. Ordering the wrong generation is the dominant failure mode reported in this category.</p>

        <h3>Engineered Now &quot;The Original Headrest&quot; HW — for Aeron Remastered (2016+)</h3>
        <p><strong>Fits:</strong> Remastered Aeron, Sizes A/B/C &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Finish:</strong> Onyx &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Price:</strong> ~$150–190</p>
        <p>This is the most consistently referenced Aeron headrest in ergonomics communities and the safest starting point if the chair is a Remastered Aeron. The mount clamps to the upper back frame rather than bonding to the mesh, so the load path runs into the frame — structurally the correct approach, since mesh is a tension membrane and shouldn't carry a cantilevered point load. Published adjustment covers vertical travel in the 3"–4" range plus a tilt arc, which on a Size C should place the pad near the 34" occiput target for a 6'4" user once the frame's own 24"–25" starting height is added.</p>
        <p>For a Size C owner at 6'4", the arithmetic is favorable: backrest top around 24"–25", occiput around 34", leaving a 9"–10" rise. That is the smallest gap of the three chairs covered here, which is why Aeron owners generally have the easiest retrofit.</p>

