        <p>If you're still deciding whether the Aeron itself fits a tall frame before adding accessories, start with the <a href="/chairs/herman-miller-aeron/tall-people/" class="link-internal">Aeron for tall people fit analysis →</a> or the full <a href="/review/aeron-size-c/" class="link-internal">Aeron Size C review →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Steelcase Leap V2 Headrest Options</h2>
        <p>The Leap V2 has the largest gap of the three chairs: a backrest topping out around 23" above the seat against a 34" occiput target at 6'4". That is roughly eleven inches of unsupported spine, and it is why Leap owners search for headrests more than Aeron owners do.</p>

        <h3>Atlas Headrest for Steelcase Leap V2 — real product, currently unbuyable</h3>
        <p><strong>Fits:</strong> Steelcase Leap V2 only &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Price:</strong> ~$190 &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Status:</strong> sold out at every verified seller as of August 2026</p>
        <p>The engineering is the right answer to the problem. The mount attaches to the Leap's upper back structure, which matters because the Leap's back is a flexing composite shell — the Steelcase &quot;Liveback&quot; system — that changes shape as you recline. A headrest bolted into a flexing shell has to tolerate that motion without loosening, so a frame-referenced clamp is the correct approach.</p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-6 text-sm text-amber-900">
          <p class="font-semibold mb-2">Availability check, August 2026 — do not buy on a stale link</p>
          <p class="mb-2">Every seller verified for this guide is out of stock. Atlas Headrest direct lists it &quot;sold out.&quot; Crandall Office Furniture, which co-brands the same unit, lists it &quot;currently unavailable.&quot; Office Logix Shop lists its competing Leap V2 headrest as backordered. The Amazon listing that previously carried it no longer resolves.</p>
          <p>Any site still presenting a live &quot;buy now&quot; button for this product is running an unchecked affiliate link. There is nothing to purchase here right now, so this guide is not going to hand you a button that earns a commission and wastes your click.</p>
        </div>

        <p>If you want to track it, Atlas sells direct at <a href="https://atlasheadrest.com/products/headrest-for-steelcase-leap-chair" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">atlasheadrest.com</a> and quotes 1&ndash;2 business days on the black frame once stock returns. That is an unaffiliated link — this guide earns nothing from it.</p>

        <h3>The Leap Plus has no headrest option at all</h3>
        <p>This is the part that matters most for tall buyers, because the Leap Plus is the model most often recommended to people over 6'2&quot; — it carries the 500 lb frame and the taller seat-height range. Atlas states plainly that the headrest &quot;does not fit Leap V1 or Leap Plus versions,&quot; and Crandall confirms it is &quot;not compatible with any variation of the V1 Leap or Leap Plus.&quot;</p>
        <p>The Leap Plus uses a different back structure to carry its higher weight rating, and the mounting geometry the V2 headrest depends on is not present. So the chair with the best raw dimensions for a tall user is also the chair with no retrofit path to head support. If recline-time neck support is a hard requirement, that is a genuine argument against the Leap Plus at purchase time — see the <a href="/review/leap-plus/" class="link-internal">Leap Plus analysis →</a> for the full dimensional trade-off.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Steelcase Gesture Headrest Gap — Stated Plainly</h2>
        <p>There is no confirmed aftermarket headrest for the Steelcase Gesture on Amazon. I'm not going to route you to a substitute product to fill the space, because there isn't one I can verify.</p>
        <p>Here is the actual situation. Steelcase offers a Gesture headrest, but only as a factory-configured option selected at the time of chair purchase. It mounts to a specific upper back assembly, and a Gesture ordered without that option does not have the structure to accept it later. This is not a bolt-on accessory — it's a different back build. If you bought a Gesture without the headrest, as I did, retrofitting through normal retail channels is not available.</p>
        <p>The remaining options are both bad. Universal clamp-on headrests marketed as fitting &quot;any office chair&quot; exist, but their mounting straps and bar clamps are designed around flat-topped backrests, and the Gesture's upper back is a contoured shell. I have not tested one and will not recommend one on unverified fitment. The other option is behavioral: recline less, or add head support from outside the chair entirely.</p>
        <p>What I do instead, at 6'4" in my own Gesture, is treat it as an upright-work chair and accept the limit. The back-tilt tension is set firm enough that I don't drift into a deep recline during long sessions. It works, but it is a workaround, not a fix. If head support during recline is a requirement for you, that is a legitimate reason to weigh a Leap V2 or an Aeron over a Gesture at purchase time — or to order a Gesture with the factory headrest from the start.</p>
        <p>My full breakdown of living with this chair at 6'4" is in the <a href="/review/gesture/" class="link-internal">Steelcase Gesture review →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How to Measure Whether You Need a Headrest</h2>
        <p>Four steps, one tape measure, and a helper. Do this before you spend $190.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Set the chair to your correct seat height.</strong> Feet flat on the floor, thighs parallel or declining slightly. Measuring at the wrong seat height corrupts every number that follows.</li>
          <li><strong>Measure the backrest top above the compressed seat surface.</strong> Sit in the chair so the cushion compresses, then measure straight up to the highest point of the backrest. Expect roughly 23" on a Leap V2, 22.5"–23" on a Gesture, 24"–25" on an Aeron Size C.</li>
          <li><strong>Measure your seated occiput height.</strong> The occiput is the bony ridge at the base of the skull, about 5"–6" below the crown. Have someone measure from the seat pan up to it. Expect about 33" at 6'2", 34" at 6'4", 35" at 6'6".</li>
          <li><strong>Subtract.</strong> Occiput height minus backrest top height equals the rise the headrest must deliver. Compare that against the product's published vertical travel plus its fixed mounting offset. If your required rise exceeds what the headrest can reach, the pad will land on your neck instead of your skull — which is worse than no headrest at all.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>A headrest contacting the cervical spine rather than the occiput pushes the neck into extension and concentrates pressure on soft tissue. That is the specific failure mode tall users report most often when a headrest is technically compatible but dimensionally too short.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What a Headrest Will Not Fix</h2>
        <p>A headrest is a narrow tool. It solves head support during recline. It does not solve any of the following, and buying one to address them will disappoint you.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Seat depth that's too short.</strong> If the seat pan doesn't reach far enough under a long femur, that's a thigh-support and popliteal-pressure problem. No headrest affects it.</li>
          <li><strong>Armrests that sit too low.</strong> Unsupported forearms load the upper trapezius continuously. That's an armrest height problem, and it produces shoulder and neck symptoms that a headrest will not touch.</li>
          <li><strong>Lumbar support positioned too low.</strong> Tall users often need lumbar support 1"–2" higher than default. A headrest does nothing for pelvic tilt or lumbar lordosis.</li>
          <li><strong>A monitor mounted too low.</strong> If the screen sits below eye level, you'll flex your neck forward regardless of what's behind your head — and the headrest becomes a thing you never touch. Fix the screen height first. See the <a href="/monitor-arm-tall-people/" class="link-internal">monitor arm height guide for tall people →</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>In practice, monitor height and armrest height are more common root causes of tall-user neck pain than the absent headrest is. Order of operations matters: screen, then armrests, then headrest.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Do tall people need a chair headrest?</h3>
        <p>Tall users benefit from a headrest primarily during recline, not during upright typing. The Leap V2, Gesture, and Aeron all top out at roughly 23"–25" above the seat pan, while seated occiput height runs about 33" at 6'2", 34" at 6'4", and 35" at 6'6". That leaves 8"–11" of unsupported cervical spine. A headrest closes that span once the chair reclines past roughly 110 degrees.</p>

        <h3>Does the Steelcase Leap V2 have a headrest?</h3>
        <p>The standard Leap V2 ships without one. Its backrest reaches about 23" above the seat pan, landing near the shoulder blades at 6'4". The Atlas Headrest for Steelcase Leap V2 (around $190) is the primary aftermarket retrofit, but it is sold out at every verified seller as of August 2026 and it does not fit the Leap Plus or Leap V1. Only the Herman Miller Aeron currently has aftermarket headrests in stock.</p>

        <h3>Is there an aftermarket headrest for the Steelcase Gesture?</h3>
        <p>No confirmed aftermarket headrest for the Steelcase Gesture is available on Amazon as of August 2026. Steelcase sells a Gesture headrest only as a factory-configured option bundled at the time of chair purchase, mounted to a specific upper back assembly. A Gesture bought without that option cannot be upgraded through a standard retail accessory. This is a genuine product gap, not an oversight in this guide.</p>

        <h3>Which headrest fits a Herman Miller Aeron?</h3>
        <p>Herman Miller does not make an Aeron headrest in any size. Engineered Now sells The Original Headrest in an HW version for the Remastered Aeron built from 2016 onward (ASIN B0B86SYST5, roughly $150–$190) and an H4 version for the Aeron Classic from 1994 to 2016 (ASIN B00MV8C8TA, roughly $140–$170). Atlas offers a Graphite Aeron headrest (ASIN B00L2IJNMK, around $190). Matching Classic versus Remastered generation is the critical fitment step.</p>

        <h3>How high should a chair headrest be for a 6'4" person?</h3>
        <p>The pad should meet the occiput — the bony ridge at the base of the skull — not the crown and not the neck. At 6'4" that target sits roughly 33"–34" above the seat pan when upright, and shifts 2"–4" lower relative to the backrest as the chair reclines. Subtract the backrest top height (about 23"–25" on these chairs) from the occiput target to get the required rise: typically 9"–11".</p>

        <h3>Will a universal clamp-on headrest work on a premium task chair?</h3>
        <p>Universal headrests are designed around flat-topped backrests with a uniform upper rail. The Gesture, Leap V2, and Aeron all use contoured or flexing upper back structures, so strap-and-bar mounts seat inconsistently and can loosen as the back flexes during recline. Purpose-built headrests reference the chair frame rather than the backrest surface, which is why they cost $140–$190 rather than $30.</p>
      </section>

